nodecode-kanban-stream is a rewrite of mbgulden's mbgulden/hermes-kanban-stream:
https://github.com/mbgulden/hermes-kanban-stream
commit d6c3e802eefb6ec1e09ad002a1bf7d45b13104a8
MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 Michael Gulden (LICENSE)

The original was a plugin for another agent harness: it registered six hooks
around a kanban worker (a tool call, its result, a model round, a request's
usage, a task's completion and its blocking), held one message per task in
Telegram, rewrote it on a throttle with the tool calls, the elapsed time and
the tokens a second, and ended with one rewrite that it pinned. It also
shipped a companion daemon that long-polled Telegram for `/steer` and posted
the instruction as a task comment.

This rewrite keeps that whole shape on Nodecode's seams: the turn of a
session replaces the kanban task, and one :FRAME hook reading the durable
facts (turn.tool_call_started, turn.usage, turn.completed / turn.failed /
turn.cancelled) replaces the six callbacks. A message is still opened by the
first tool call, still rewritten on a throttle into the same message, still
finished once and pinned; the token, chat and allowlist come from the cell's
own config section rather than from environment variables and a secret
scope.

Left out: the `/steer` companion daemon and the `[steer]` comment watch —
Nodecode's own `steer_turn` submits a prompt into a running session, so a
second long-poll that wrote comments would duplicate the harness; steering
from a chat is what the telegram channel cell already does. Also left out:
the `[no-stream]` body marker (the `sessions` list says which sessions
stream), the SQLite reads of the original's board (Nodecode's session store
is the harness's, not a cell's), and the Hermes secret-scope integration.
